{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1784301159,"nonce":"avDh/39x07Vuv9DJP2ngSk0EgGI=","title":"[dogfood] run_as_root steps cannot write the CAS-restored /workspace (DAC_OVERRIDE dropped)","message":"A stock image whose USER is root (e.g. `rust:1-bookworm`) forces `security.run_as_root: true`, because the non-root baseline sets only runAsNonRoot (no runAsUser) and the pod is rejected for running as root otherwise.\n\nBut the hardened baseline drops ALL capabilities incl. CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, and the workspace restored from the clone's CAS snapshot is owned by the clone image's uid (65532). So a run_as_root (uid 0) step can READ /workspace but CANNOT create files there:\n\n    error: Permission denied (os error 13) at path \"/workspace/targetOSSVPz\"   (cargo)\n\ni.e. the single most common CI shape — clone, then build IN the workspace — fails under run_as_root with a stock root image. fsGroup=65532 is set on the pod, but the tar-restore of the workspace does not leave the dir group-writable/setgid, so group membership doesn't help.\n\nWorkaround used in the dogfood pipeline: point the build's writable output dir at a container-owned path (CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/...). But that's non-obvious and per-tool.\n\nOptions to consider: (a) restore the workspace group-writable + setgid so any fsGroup member (incl. run_as_root) can write; (b) let run_as_root steps run as the workspace-owning uid; (c) allow a step to request an explicit runAsUser; (d) document the constraint prominently.\n\nPositive: verified run_as_root IS honored on the trigger/YAML path (runAsUser:0, runAsNonRoot:false) while the rest of the baseline holds (drop ALL caps, no-privesc, seccomp RuntimeDefault) — the 'create_run ignores run_as_root' bug is API-path-specific.","files":null}]}